  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="pod" class="article-section article-section-wide">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.1 · the complete deployment shape</span>
        <h2>Everything with authority is visible in the pod.</h2>
        <p>The default chart creates no Service or Ingress. The broker’s only control endpoint is a <code>0660</code> socket in a <code>0710</code> broker-owned directory on a memory-backed volume; the gateway can connect to it and can’t replace it. An opt-in ClusterIP can target a gateway webhook; it never exposes the broker.</p>
      </div>

      <figure class="kubernetes-cutaway">
        <div class="k8s-cutaway-title"><span>Chart {{ release.chart.publishedVersion }} · app {{ release.chart.publishedAppVersion }} · replicas: 1 · Recreate</span><code>runAsUser: 65532 · 65533</code></div>
        <div class="k8s-init-row">
          <article><small>init container · root + CHOWN/FOWNER only</small><strong>prepare-files</strong><span>projected inputs → real 0600 files, the broker’s as 65532 and the gateway’s as 65533</span></article>
          <i aria-hidden="true">finishes before daemons start ↓</i>
        </div>
        <div class="k8s-runtime-row">
          <article class="k8s-gateway-box"><small>regular container · UID 65533</small><strong>dekopond</strong><ul><li>Slack / Discord / Telegram / WhatsApp</li><li>model client</li><li>bounded sessions</li><li>no provider credential</li></ul></article>
          <div class="k8s-socket-box"><span>memory-backed emptyDir</span><strong>broker.sock</strong><code>0660 · group 65534</code><i aria-hidden="true">proposal →<br>← result</i></div>
          <article class="k8s-broker-box"><small>native sidecar · UID 65532</small><strong>dekopon-brokerd</strong><ul><li>Cedar policy</li><li>constraint sets</li><li>provider credentials</li><li>Wasm + audit records</li></ul></article>
        </div>
        <div class="k8s-volume-row">
          <div><small>memory</small><strong>/etc/dekopon</strong><span>config · policy · provider credentials</span></div>
          <div><small>image</small><strong>/opt/dekopon/providers</strong><span>owner-matched Wasm components</span></div>
          <div><small>retained claims</small><strong>/var/lib/dekopon*</strong><span>optional provider storage · ChatGPT credential</span></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>One image supplies both commands. Separate containers and separate UIDs keep the roles apart; the shared filesystem namespace is what the Unix socket needs. Provider storage, when enabled, mounts only into the broker.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="one-pod" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.2 · topology follows transport</span>
        <h2>The Unix socket decides the placement.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Constraint</th><th scope="col">Kubernetes consequence</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Broker binds a filesystem Unix socket</th><td>Gateway and broker need one shared pod volume; a webhook Service doesn’t replace it.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Socket mode is <code>0660</code></th><td>The broker owns it as <code>65532</code>; the gateway, <code>65533</code>, reaches it through supplementary group <code>65534</code> and pins <code>broker.serverUid</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Peer identity uses <code>SO_PEERCRED</code></th><td>A Service or TCP proxy would discard the authenticated transport.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Provider storage has one exclusive writer</th><td>The Deployment is fixed at one replica with <code>Recreate</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Gateway probes the broker once</th><td>The broker runs as a native sidecar whose startup probe gates gateway start.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Two images would still need the same application version and the same pod, so the chart ships one image with both executables. Each container picks its command and keeps its own job.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="files" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.3 · fail-closed file hygiene</span>
        <h2>A Kubernetes Secret mount isn’t a broker credential file.</h2>
        <p>Projected ConfigMap and Secret volumes are symlink farms owned by root. The broker deliberately refuses symlinks, wrong owners, multiple links, and unsafe mode bits.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="file-materialization">
        <div><small>Kubernetes projection</small><strong><code>key → ..data/key</code></strong><span>root-owned symlink · rejected</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">init container copies →</i>
        <div><small>private emptyDir</small><strong>regular file</strong><span>one link · the daemon’s own UID · mode 0600</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">daemon opens →</i>
        <div><small>startup validation</small><strong>serve or fail closed</strong><span>never “warn and continue”</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mounted shape</th><th scope="col">Why the broker refuses it</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Secret / ConfigMap volume</th><td><code>O_NOFOLLOW</code> stops at the projection symlink.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>subPath</code></th><td>The file is real but root-owned and normally <code>0644</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>fsGroup</code></th><td>Group readability is exactly what the credentials file rejects.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Init-container copy</th><td>Produces a fresh owner-only regular file that passes every check.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>The projected source is visible only to <code>prepare-files</code>. The two daemons mount the materialized output, not the Kubernetes projection.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="state" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.4 · what persists</span>
        <h2>Audit leaves the pod; provider storage stays.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="audit-volume-map">
        <div><span>every decision</span><strong>broker.decision</strong><small>log record on the message’s trace</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">stdout always →</i>
        <div><span>with telemetry</span><strong>OTLP logs</strong><small>the same record, exported</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">retained by →</i>
        <div><span>your receiver</span><strong>OpenObserve</strong><small>outside the pod</small></div>
      </div>

      <p>There is no audit volume. The audit record is the log record the broker emits inside the trace, so a pod restart keeps exactly what the telemetry receiver kept; a deployment that wants audit past a restart configures <code>telemetry</code>. Optional provider storage uses one retained claim mounted only into the broker. Storage names are unkeyed SHA-256, so there is no key Secret to retain, and a corrupt namespace is reset by the grant that finds it rather than quarantined at startup.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Size the storage volume before creating it.</strong>
        <p>The target single-node storage class can’t expand. Inactive namespace generations stay charged to the root quota until you remove them offline with the daemons stopped.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="startup" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.5 · ordering without fake HTTP health</span>
        <h2>A real broker request is the broker readiness check.</h2>
      </div>

      <ol class="mental-model-list compact-steps">
        <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Materialize files.</strong><p>The init container copies, owns, modes, and verifies each required file.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Compile providers.</strong><p>The broker validates configuration, Cedar, credentials, and every component before binding. The chart refuses to render a broker whose identities don’t map its own probe UID.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Answer a real request.</strong><p>The startup probe runs <code>dekopon-brokerd probe --socket</code> across the real socket and peer checks, loading no credentials or telemetry.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>04</span><div><strong>Start the gateway.</strong><p>Kubernetes starts the regular container only after the native broker sidecar passes startup.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Drain in reverse.</strong><p>Gateway sessions stop first. The broker then finishes in-flight effects and flushes its exporters.</p></div></li>
      </ol>

      <p>There is no liveness probe. An automatic restart could kill a broker mid-invocation and would not repair an external dependency. Readiness reports truth without manufacturing that risk.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="security" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.6 · pod security posture</span>
        <h2>Root exists for one bounded setup job.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-label-cards">
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Daemons</span></div><h3>Non-root and read-only</h3><p>UIDs 65532 and 65533, no added capabilities, no privilege escalation, read-only root filesystems, and RuntimeDefault seccomp.</p></article>
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Init</span></div><h3>Root with two capabilities</h3><p><code>CHOWN</code> and <code>FOWNER</code> create the ownership the daemons insist on. Everything else is dropped.</p></article>
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-direction"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Current limit</span></div><h3>A gateway lane, not tenant isolation</h3><p>A compromised gateway can still vouch for any subject inside its attestor grant. Broker-UID or host compromise is outside the boundary.</p></article>
      </div>

      <p>Wasmtime JIT compilation requires executable memory operations allowed by RuntimeDefault seccomp. A hand-written tighter profile must prove a real component still loads.</p>
    </section>

